{"kind":"report","text":"Decision for1b848bb0: approve G0's bounded object/head/tree interface and bounded live-item query direction. G1 and directory lifetime need the explicit contracts below before their dependent behavior changes. This answers the pre-Source diagnosis; e029/cc570904 and all actual implementation/provider duties remain open.\n\nSource diagnosis confirmed at exact Main0157307b9. ArtifactsProvider.objects194-207 collects copies around Reader.closure. Reader.object202-221 checks type, size and Git hash; closure283-312 walks all reachable parents/trees/blobs. SmartHttpSource.object419-440 requests want+done, decodes/caches its full bounded pack, then applies the requested object's return allowance. Removing the outer collection cannot remove that transport cost. sendOnce194-237 checks supplied-memory closure, stops at the old parent, and still walks the new tree. Reads.summary198-228 collects each type's live max; items231-241 pages only final items. Directory lane189-191 permits100000 live rows. CLI issuesOf1739-1759 collects all directory rows and reads each lane's whole summary, including its other live items. Sqlite.page177-185 already uses type/state/ID index ranges; reuse this boundary rather than adding a full-table filter.\n\nG0: expose explicitly named verification facts: exact object format, repository identity, commit bytes/hash/type, parent IDs, root tree and the objects actually read. A verified commit/tree is not verified reachable history. Use a bounded source and bounded path editor, preserving raw Git name bytes, modes, ordering, collision/depth rules and untouched sibling IDs. Charge aggregate bytes, objects, depth, transport/inflation/cache copies and time before allocation; an object-count bound alone does not bound memory. A transport must advertise/implement the exact bounded retrieval contract. Its refusal must remain truthful unsupported/too-large/unavailable, with no full-pack fallback labelled bounded. No new public export or source dependency cycle is needed solely to hide this change.\n\nG1: record where the proof that a base is already held comes from. Bind it to immutable base commit/tree, object format and actual destination repository; a supplied ID, shallow want or successful commit read is insufficient to inherit an existing whole-closure claim. Resolve needed base objects by immutable ID and verify their types/hashes/sizes. New output contains the new commit and changed path trees/blobs; do not rebuild a full-tree/full-history array merely to send a smaller pack. Preserve expected parent/tree, destination/ref identity, CAS old value, final grant/token recheck and all custody duties. Current sendOnce accepts only a complete own push answer plus matching readback: a lost own reply remains unknown even when the target ref matches. Do not import the archive's separate readback reconciliation semantics into publication.\n\nCurrent founding/staging/checker/ancestry paths that require closure must retain that guarantee or use a separately reviewed versioned verification contract. G0 alone does not permit silently weakening first-head adoption. Keep exact checker snapshots and ancestry proof independent. Before Source changes that replace closure, state the narrower fact each consumer needs, the retained-base/host-possession premise, failure/restart behavior and a distinguishing witness. If the production host cannot implement bounded transport yet, deliver the pure path/transport seam and truthful unsupported result without claiming the import bottleneck is solved. Preserve full functionality as an outstanding duty.\n\nBounded query: add an authorized type/state/ID-ordered query with server-enforced row AND response-byte/work limits. Bind cursor to full scope incarnation, exact head, query/filter/order and continuation position. Each request is authenticated under its current grant/session; signed reads must bind the exact canonical query/cursor/head rather than reuse an unrelated summary authorization. With no retained snapshot, reject/restart explicitly on head drift; never concatenate pages from different heads as a complete snapshot. An oversized single item must have a defined refusal/projection, not an endless empty continuation or implicit truncation. Complete means exhausted at that exact query/head; filtered counts and cross-scope observations must name their limits.\n\nConsumers must remain bounded too: Page/MCP expose continuation; CLI streams or pages rows and marks partial totals, without collecting every page into issuesOf's all array. Point lookup of an issue must avoid scanning every issue. Do not read every lane's whole summary just to get its intent: use an authorized bounded singleton/item query for the selected lanes. Directory indexes can lag (commands.ts1734-1737 notes closes sends no update), so directory pagination alone is not authoritative current issue state. Retain exact per-lane head/qualification when refreshing, and do not claim an atomic room-wide snapshot. Cover no gaps/duplicates at a stable head, head drift, revocation, byte limits and a large unrelated comment set with bounded work.\n\nDirectory lifetime: scopeEntries is cumulative; archives do not reclaim or reset it. Until a reviewed successor/partition protocol exists, enforce current admission with explicit scope-full and truthful supported envelope, preserving reserved settlement entries. Do not raise constants or reset/move history. Planner must own the new versioned protocol before lifetime-extension Source: partition identities and lookup, unique numbering/source mapping, creation/rollover authority, atomic handoff and late deliveries, cumulative capacity for routing/control histories, retained old refs/replay and current membership/rules/destination bindings. Partitioning rows while one root records every update just moves the bottleneck. Existing pins receive no inferred migration. This report selects bounded refusal as the interim behavior; it does not select an unreviewed successor algorithm.\n\nValidation needed at the eventual Source head: real local Git path edits preserve untouched bytes/modes and exact IDs with corrupt/missing-base, stale ref, lost reply and revoked-grant controls; instrument transport/object reads to show unrelated ancestry/tree bytes are not loaded, beside explicit envelope refusal. Use real native query/storage scenarios and meaningful consumer witnesses, then one gate. No implementation, tests, gate, provider operation or measurements were run for this static decision. Architecture: separate verified-head, closure, snapshot and ancestry claims. Security: preserve authenticated queries and publication own-answer custody. Simplification: reuse indexed pages and current read/judgment boundaries; add no alternate authority or history reset.","body":{"formal_source_approval":"false","head":"0157307b904c9afc75500b626fc565a152113cfa","review_scope":"design-decision-only"}}